  "title": "Slope and Rate of Change",
  "body": " Slope and Rate of Change  Calculating Rate of Change  The graph shows how the thickness of a typical land-based glacier has changed over 43 years. Graph of thickness on [1960,2040]x[0,100]   At first glance, what does the graph tell you about the glaciers?  What was the total change, , in thickness from 1960 to 2003?    Year,  Thickness,  1960   2003      Calculate the average yearly change in thickness, over that time interval. Give units with your answers.  The graph appears to be almost linear from 1992 to 2002. Read the graph to complete the table.    Year,  Thickness,  1992   2002    Graph of thickness on [1992,2002]x[91,96]  Calculate the slope of the graph from 1992 to 2002. Include units in your answer.  Compare the average yearly change you calculated in part (c) with the slope you calculated in part (e). What do those values tell us about land-based glaciers?     Interpreting Slope as a Rate  The amount of salt that can be dissolved in a beaker of water depends upon the temperature of the water. The table shows the amounts for several temperatures.    Temperature, C,  10  12  15  21  25  40  52  Salt, (g),  33  34  35.5  38.5  40.5  48  54    Which variable is the input, and which is the output?  input: output:  If you plot the data, which variable goes on the horizontal axis, and which on the vertical?  horizontal: vertical:  Without plotting the points, how can you decide if they will lie on a straight line? Do that.   Describe the slope of the line as a rate of change. Include units in your answer. What does the slope tell you about dissolving salt?  Use the slope to answer the question: If you increase the temperature 5°C from the current temperature, how much more salt will dissolve?     Working with Formulas   We now have several new formulas to work with. Solve each formula for the indicated variable:      Suppose you are studying the graph of a line, . Explain the difference between what the statements and mean.   Explain how to find from a graph of .    Checkpoint   What mathematical concept did you study in Activity 1?  When you calculate slope, does the change in the input variable appear in the numerator or the denominator?  What should you include in your calculation of slope to help you interpret it as a rate of change?  Without graphing, how can you tell whether data points lie on a straight line?  How does function notation appear in the formula for calculating slope?    "
