  "number": "3.3",
  "title": "Roots and Radicals",
  "body": " Roots and Radicals  Maximum Speed for Ships  When a ship moves through the water, it creates waves that hinder its own progress. Because of this resistance, there is an upper limit to the speed at which a ship can travel. This maximum speed, or hull velocity, is given in knots by where is the length of the vessel in feet.   Complete the table of values for as a function of . (We'll get to later.)  Tell students that we are not using the third row of the table ( ) until later in the problem.    (feet)  0  200  400  600  800  1000  (knots)        (knots)         Graph maximum speed against vessel length. Which basic graph will your graph resemble?  grid [0,1000]x[0,50]  Write and solve an equation to answer the question: What is the length of a vessel whose top speed is 20 knots? Locate the corresponding point on your graph.   The largest ship ever built, the oil tanker Seawise Giant, was 1504 feet long. What was its top speed?  Write the formula for using a decimal fraction for the exponent.  Write the formula for using a radical.  As a ship approaches its maximum speed, the power required increases sharply. Therefore, most merchant ships are designed to cruise at speeds no higher than . Complete the table of values in part (a) for .  Graph the cruising speed on the same axes with .  What was the cruising speed of the Seawise Giant? What percent of its maximum speed is that?    Heart Rate  An animal's heart rate is related to its size or mass, with smaller animals generally having faster heart rates. The heart rates of mammals are given approximately by the power function where is the animal's mass in kilograms and is a constant.    A typical human male weighs about 70 kilograms and has a resting heart rate of 70 beats per minute. Find the constant of proportionality, , and write a formula for as a function of .  Complete the table with the heart rates of the mammals whose masses are given.    Animal  Cat  Wolf  Horse  Polar Bear  Elephant  Mass (kg)  5  40  400  1500  5400  Heart Rate        Sketch a graph of for masses up to 6000 kilograms.  grid [0,6000]x[0,80]  What would be the mass of a mammal whose heart rate is 40 beats per minute?   Write the formula for using a decimal fraction for the exponent.  Write the formula for using a radical.    Laws of Exponents    Use exponents to explain why .  Use exponents to explain why .  Use exponents to explain why .   Explain in words how to evaluate each expression. Then illustrate with .          Which of the following statements are true for all positive values of the variables? Try several numberical values to verify your answers.   ?  ?  ?  ?  ?  ?  ?  ?   Solve each equation.          Checkpoint   Delbert says his calculator told him that is equal to 2.5. What did he do wrong?  What does the notation mean? How is it evaluated?  Express each of the following algebraic notations in words, and evaluate each for :  In Activity 2, explain why the graph of is decreasing.   "
