  "number": "4.3",
  "title": "Logarithms",
  "body": " Logarithms  Solving Equations   Compare the two equations below. The first is a quadratic equation, and the second is an exponential equation. You can probably solve both of these equations in your head. Solutions:  Let's analyze how we get the solutions. In the first equation, is squared, so we perform the opposite operation to solve, or take the square root of both sides:  In the second equation, is an exponent. We ask ourselves \"What power do we need on base 2 to get 8?\" Finding a power is called taking a logarithm.  Now compare the two equations below. One is cubic and one is exponential: These are not so easy to solve in your head. But we use the same techniques as before: But this time, we use a calculator to evaluate the answers. Locate the log button on your calculator. (You can check both of these solutions by plugging them into the original equations.)  The solution of the first equation answers the question \"What number must I cube to get 17?\"  The solution of the second equation answers the question \"To what power must I raise 10 to get 135?\"    Definition of Logarithm  We use logarithms to solve exponential equations.  means \"to what power must I raise to get ?\"  For example, because    Fill in the blanks.   because  because  because   From the examples, we see that a logarithm is just an exponent. In fact, Convert each exponential equation to logarithmic form.            Logarithms and Inverse Operations  We use inverse operations to solve equations. For example, cubing and taking cube roots are inverse operations. Each operation undoes the effect of the other.  Start with any number, say 5, cube it, and then take the cube root. We get back to the original number: We can write the two operations in one step, like this: . The same thing works for any number , so .   Here are some more examples of inverse operations. Fill in the blanks.      Logarithms and exponentials are inverse operations. Taking a logarithm base is the opposite of evaluating an exponential function with base .  For example, let , and start by start by evaluating with . Then take the log base 3 of the result: Or, . So we have the rule:  Fill in the blanks.      We use logarithms to solve exponential equations. Notice that the solution, , is the exponent in the original equation. Once again, we see that a logarithm is just an exponent; in this case it is the exponent on base 5 that will give us 125.  You can think of taking a logarithm as a command that says \"find me the exponent!\"  Logarithms base 10 are called common logarithms, because they are used more often in applications than logs to other bases. We often omit the base on common logs, so that  Evaluate the logs:      Estimate each log between two integers. Then use a calculator to evaluate the log.  3(e) It may help to list powers of 10 on the board and locate numbers between powers of 10.          Solving Base 10 Equations  We can now solve exponential equations that have base 10, using the fact that . Here is an example. Logarithms are very sensitive to round-off error. You should keep four places of accuracy in your answers.   Follow the steps to solve each equation.    Take logs of both sides:  Simplify each side:  Solve for :    Isolate the power:  Take logs of both sides:  Simplify each side:  Solve for :     Atmospheric Pressure  The atmospheric pressure decreases with altitude above the surface of the earth, according to the formula where is altitude in miles and is atmospheric pressure in inches of mercury.   Use technology to graph this function for .   Use algebra to answer the question, then verify on your graph:  The elevation of Mount McKinley, the highest mountain in the United Sates, is 20,320 feet. What is the atmospheric pressure at the top? (Hint: 1 mile = 5280 feet.)  Use algebra to answer the question, then verify on your graph:  How high above sea level is the atmospheric pressure 16.1 inches of mercury?    Checkpoint   What is the only difference in the steps for solving the two equations:  Compute and . Are they the same?  What happens when you apply an operation to a number, then apply the inverse operation?  Explain how to use the graph of to estimate the value of .   "
