  "title": "Curve Fitting",
  "body": " Curve Fitting  Using Three Points  You are driving at 60 miles per hour when you step on the brakes. Find a quadratic formula, , for the distance in feet that your car has traveled seconds after braking.  1a. Check that students understand that the unknowns in this system are , , and .    (sec)      (feet)        Use the first three data points from the table to write three equations.  Simplify the equations, and write a system.  Solve the system.   State your formula for .    Finding the Equation of a Parabola  We now have three forms for the equation of a parabola:   standard form  factored form  vertex form    Each of these forms is useful for highlighting different properties of the parabola.   What is the -intercept of the graph of ?  What are the -intercepts of the graph of ?  What is the vertex of the graph of ?   How can we find the equation of a parabola?   If we know the -intercepts of the parabola, then we use the factored form.  If we know the vertex, we use the vertex form.  In each case, we must know one other point on the parabola in order to find the value of .    Problem 1   Write an equation for a parabola that has -intercepts at and . (There are many possible answers.)  Write an equation for another parabola that has the same -intercepts.  Write an equation for the parabola that has -intercepts at and and -intercept at .   Problem 2   Write an equation for a parabola whose vertex is the point . (Many answers are possible.)  Find the value of if the -intercept of the parabola is 18.   Problem 3  Find an equation for each parabola. Use the vertex form or the factored form of the equation, whichever is more appropriate.   Parabola on [-2,14]x[-2,18] vertex at (6,2) y-intercept at (0,14)  Parabola on [-6,8]x[-18,6] vertex at (1,-16) x-intercept3 at -3 and 5    Curve-Fitting with the Vertex Form  Francine is designing a synchronized fountain display for a hotel in Las Vegas. For each fountain, water emerges in a parabolic arc from a nozzle three feet above the ground. Francine would like the vertex of the arc to be eight feet high, and two feet horizontally from the nozzle.  water from fountain forming parabola   Choose a coordinate system with its origin at the base of the fountain. Label the coordinates of two points on the path of the water.  Write an equation for the path of the water.  How far from the base of the nozzle will the stream of water hit the ground?    Checkpoint   What were the variables in your 3x3 system in Activity 1?  Which variable did you eliminate first from the system in Activity 1?  If you use the last three points in the table instead of the first three points, will you get the same regression equation? Why or why not?  Name three forms for quadratic equations.    "
